{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.72,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "polarity",
      "components": ["T5"],
      "pins": ["T5.2", "T5.3"],
      "nets": ["/+24V_F", "+24V"],
      "observed": "T5 is an AON6407 P-channel MOSFET, but the netlist connects its source pin (T5.2) to the fused input node /+24V_F and its drain pin (T5.3) to the board +24V rail. The gate network (R10 and D1) is referenced to /+24V_F.",
      "expected": "For a P-channel MOSFET used as reverse-polarity protection, the source should be on the protected load side (+24V) and the drain on the input side (/+24V_F), so the body diode points from input to output and blocks reverse polarity/backfeed. The source/drain connection in T5 is reversed for this standard topology.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"T5\") (value \"AON6407\") ... (description \"P-Channel 30 V 32A 10mΩ AON6407\")"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"6\") (name \"/+24V_{F}\") ... (node (ref \"T5\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"S\") ...)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"3\") (name \"+24V\") ... (node (ref \"T5\") (pin \"3\") (pinfunction \"D\") ...)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "README states the PCB includes \"reverse polarity protection, TVS diodes, proper isolation\""}
      ],
      "impact": "With correct polarity the rail may still power up, but the protection function is compromised: on reversed input the P-MOSFET body diode can conduct, and when the input is removed the +24V rail capacitors can backfeed through the body diode into the input connector.",
      "confidence": 0.72
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files otherwise match the specification, but the AON6407 reverse-polarity MOSFET T5 appears to have its source and drain connected opposite the standard P-channel reverse-polarity topology, making the input protection ineffective under reverse or removed-input conditions."
}

